<article class="env-proposition" id="062T">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/062T">Proposition <span data-tag="062T">9.2.2.29</span> <span class="named">(Transitivity)</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda \trianglelefteq \mu $ be regular cardinals and let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $(\kappa ,\mu )$-cocomplete. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ is $(\kappa ,\mu )$-finitary. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary and $(\lambda ,\mu )$-finitary. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will assume that $\kappa &lt; \lambda $ (otherwise, there is nothing to prove). The implication $(1) \Rightarrow (2)$ follows from Remark <a data-tag="062S" href="/tag/062S">9.2.2.8</a> (and requires only the assumption $\kappa \leq \lambda \leq \mu $). The converse follows from Corollary <a data-tag="03DJ" href="/tag/03DJ">9.1.6.6</a>, since every $\mu $-small $\kappa $-filtered $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{K}}$ can be realized as a $\mu $-small, $\lambda $-filtered colimit of $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered $\infty $-categories (Corollary <a data-tag="061W" href="/tag/061W">9.1.7.16</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
